\title{Seed-Compressibility of Pseudorandom Streams:\\
Detection, Certified Seed Recovery, and the Capacity of a Generator Router}
\author{Aristotle}
\date{18 August 2026}

\begin{document}

\begin{abstract}
A substantial fraction of real-world data is not authored but \emph{computed}:
game worlds, simulation traces, noise textures and certain file formats are the
output of deterministic pseudorandom generators. Such a file carries no more
information than the seed that produced it. We develop the exact theory of
detecting and inverting this situation. Modelling a generator as a pair
$(\stepmap,\out)$ on a state space $S$, we call a finite word
\emph{seed-compressible} when some seed reproduces it symbol for symbol. We give
a complete analysis of the linear feedback shift register (LFSR) family: a window
lemma identifying the register state with a sliding window on its own future
output, an exact (sound \emph{and} complete) fingerprint test in the form of an
order-$L$ linear recurrence, free seed recovery (the seed is literally the first
$L$ output symbols), and the reduction of the full-output linear congruential
generator $x \mapsto ax+b$ to the order-two recurrence
$x_{t+2} = (a+1)x_{t+1} - a x_{t}$. Our main theorem is that $2L$ observed
symbols certify a recovered order-$L$ generator forever: two sequences each
satisfying \emph{some} order-$L$ linear recurrence, with possibly different tap
vectors, that agree on the first $2L$ indices agree at every index; and $2L-1$
symbols provably do not suffice. On the negative side, a router over a finite
family of generators with state spaces $S_i$ compresses at most $\sum_i |S_i|$
files of each length, so a router that compresses everything saves nothing. We
count the order-$L$ family exactly at order one, obtaining $q^2-q+1 =
(q^3+1)/(q+1)$ over a field with $q$ elements, in agreement with a conjectured
general closed form $(q^{2L+1}+1)/(q+1)$. Finally we refute the natural
noise-tolerance threshold $2L+2e+1$ for every error budget $e \ge 1$, prove the
corrected multiplicative threshold $2L(2e+1)$, and show it is sharp at order one.
\end{abstract}

